course: world
lesson: 168
title: The whole class punished, and "you didn't say no"
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Someone draws on the board while the teacher is out. Nobody owns up. "Right, the whole class stays in at break." One person did it, so twenty-nine people who did nothing get punished too. That is called collective punishment, and it is not fair to the others.
explain: A cousin of it is judging the whole group by a few: "Year 5 are the noisiest class ever", when half sat silent and two boys were noisy. A class is lots of different people.
explain: Another trap is "you didn't say no". Max asks for Freddie's rubber, Freddie is busy and doesn't hear, and Max takes it. But silence is not a yes, and a shrug is not a yes either. Only a clear yes is a yes.
explain: Some targets keep moving: Table 3 finish first, "too rushed"; they finish last, "too slow". And some words get stretched: "I'll think about it" is not "I promise".
explain: Kids push back on the reasoning, not the teacher: "Miss, Alfie sneezed. Is the zoo cancelled because of Alfie's nose?" Fair rules are clear before the start, and they are about who did what.
fact: Punishing a whole group for what one person did is called collective punishment; it is unfair to those who did nothing.
fact: Judging a whole group by a few of its members is a hasty generalisation.
fact: Silence is not a yes; grown-ups call reading a yes into silence arguing from silence.
fact: A shrug can mean "I don't know", "I'd rather not" or "maybe"; only a clear yes is a yes.
fact: You need a real yes before taking something or doing something to someone; if they don't answer, ask again or wait.
fact: Many people find it hard to say no, so it is kind to ask in a way that makes saying no easy.
fact: A threat far bigger than the thing it is about is called a disproportionate threat; a fair rule is one you can actually keep.
fact: Changing the target each time someone meets it is moving the goalposts; fair rules are clear before the race starts.
fact: Being near a problem is not the same as causing it, and the person who reports a problem often deserves thanks; blaming someone for being near is guilt by association.
fact: Pet goldfish can live 10 years or more with great care, but many die younger; 6 years is a good long life for a classroom fish.
fact: "I'll think about it" is not a promise; stretching what words mean to suit yourself is called equivocation.
fact: Words people use: "bruv" means brother or mate (London), "no cap" means honestly, "this is giving group punishment" means this looks like group punishment (young slang), "man" at the end of a sentence is just for emphasis, and "Sir" and "Miss" are what pupils call teachers in Britain.
exercise: Someone draws on the board and nobody owns up, so the whole class stays in. What is this called? | Collective punishment | A fair consequence | A hasty generalisation | Arguing from silence
exercise: Why is keeping the whole class in unfair? | Most of them did nothing wrong | Break time is too short anyway | The drawing was very small | Teachers should never punish anyone
exercise: The teacher calls Year 5 the noisiest class ever. Who was actually noisy? | Two boys | The whole class | Half the class | Nobody at all
exercise: Max takes Freddie's rubber because Freddie "didn't say no". Why didn't Freddie answer? | He was concentrating and didn't hear | He wanted Max to have it | He was too shy to say yes | He was angry with Max for taking things without asking first
exercise: Mum asks if Ben did his homework, he says nothing, and Mum says "Good." What was her mistake? | She treated silence as a yes | She asked the question too early | She didn't shout loud enough | She trusted the teacher's email
exercise: Kayla's friend shrugs when asked about the pencil case. What does a shrug mean? | It could mean "maybe" or "I'd rather not", not yes | It always means yes | It always means no | It means "take it for a week, and give it back when you're done"
exercise: What is the kind way to ask to borrow something? | Ask so that saying no is easy, and wait for a clear yes | Ask once quietly and take it if there's no answer | Take it first and then ask afterwards, once you've already used it | Ask a friend of theirs instead
exercise: The teacher says nobody goes on the trip if one more person talks, and someone coughs. What is wrong with the threat? | It is far too big for the thing and can't really be kept | It isn't big enough to work | Coughing really counts as talking | Trips should be cancelled for any noise at all, including coughs and sneezes
exercise: Table 3 are "too rushed" when first and "too slow" when last. What is this called? | Moving the goalposts | A fair rule | Arguing from silence | Collective punishment
exercise: How could the teacher make the table race fair? | Say before the start what counts, like "first to finish neatly" | Pick the winner after seeing the work | Never give out stickers again | Let Table 3 choose the rule
exercise: The teacher sends Leo to the office for being near a broken window. What had Leo done? | He reported the broken window | He broke the window | He saw who broke it and kept quiet | He was trying to fix the window
exercise: The class goldfish dies at 6 years old and the teacher blames crisps. What is most likely true? | It had a good long life and died naturally | Someone fed it crisps every day | Goldfish only live a few weeks | It died because its tank was much too big for one small goldfish
exercise: Josh said he'd "think about" tidying his room. Mum says that's the same as a promise. What is true? | "I'll think about it" is not a promise | It is exactly the same as a proper promise | It means he will definitely tidy it by 5 | It means he has already tidied it all up
exercise: How could Mum and Josh avoid the argument next time? | Make a clear deal: "Will you tidy it by 5?" | Never ask Josh to tidy again | Mum tidies the room herself | Josh should always just say "maybe" so nobody can ever be cross
exercise: What do grown-ups call blaming someone just because they were near the problem? | Guilt by association | Collective punishment | Moving the goalposts | A fair test
test: Kid at the back: "So I'm in jail for a drawing I didn't draw? This is giving group punishment, Miss." What is he objecting to? | Being punished for something someone else did | The teacher leaving the room | Not being allowed to draw | Having break at all
test: Quiet girl: "Miss, I literally haven't spoken since 9 o'clock. I've been more silent than a library." What is she showing? | The whole class was judged by a few | She is the noisiest one in the whole class | Libraries are actually very noisy places | The teacher can't hear very well at all
test: Freddie: "I didn't say no 'cause I didn't HEAR you, bruv. That's not a yes, that's me doing fractions." What does "bruv" mean? | Mate, or brother (London slang) | Teacher | Stranger | Enemy
test: Teacher's email, basically: "Ben's homework: missing." Mum: "BEN." What should Mum do next time? | Ask until she gets a real answer, or check the homework book | Take silence as a yes again | Never ask about homework | Only ask the teacher
test: Friend: "A shrug isn't a yes, Kayla! A shrug is a shrug!" Is she being unkind? | No, she's pointing out that only a clear yes counts | Yes, shrugging is rude | Yes, she should lend it anyway | No, she's agreeing with Kayla that she can keep the pencil case
test: Kid: "Miss, Alfie sneezed. Are we all cancelled? Is the zoo cancelled because of Alfie's nose?" What is he poking fun at? | A threat too big for the thing | Alfie for being ill | The zoo for being far away | Sneezing in class
test: Table 3: "First is too fast, last is too slow, what are we meant to do, finish in the middle and hope?" Which is true? | The rule should be clear before the race starts | Table 3 should always try to finish right in the middle | Stickers should only ever go to the very slowest table | There is no fair way at all to give out the stickers
test: Leo: "I'm the one who TOLD you about it, Sir. That's like blaming the fire alarm for the fire." What should the teacher have done first? | Asked Leo what he saw | Called Leo's parents straight away | Kept the whole class in | Fixed the window himself
test: Kid: "Sir, Bubbles was older than my little sister. Bubbles had a long life, man, no cap." What is the kid's point? | Six years is a long life for a classroom goldfish | Bubbles was fed crisps by his sister | Goldfish live longer than children | Somebody in the class must be found and punished for what happened to Bubbles
test: Big sister: "Josh, 'I'll think about it' is the oldest trick in the book." What trick does she mean? | Using vague words so you never really promise | Thinking very slowly on purpose | Tidying only half the room | Hiding all your toys under the bed so the room looks tidy from the door
test: What would happen if teachers always kept the whole class in when one person did something? | Lots of children who did nothing would be punished unfairly | Nobody in the class would ever misbehave again, so it would be worth it | Only the guilty child would miss break | The class would get extra break time
exam: Identify the term properly applied to the punishment of an entire class for an act committed by one unidentified pupil. | Collective punishment | Guilt by association | Moving the goalposts | Equivocation
exam: Select the response consistent with the established principles concerning the absence of a reply to a request. | Silence does not constitute consent | Silence constitutes consent in all cases | Silence constitutes refusal in all cases | Silence constitutes a binding promise
exam: Determine the most likely cause of death of a classroom goldfish aged six years, in the absence of other evidence. | Natural ageing over a long life | Habitual feeding upon crisps | Deliberate interference by a pupil | Excessive size of the aquarium
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the statement "I shall think about it" when treated as a promise? | An equivocation upon the meaning of the words | A binding and explicit undertaking | A collective punishment | An appeal to tradition
